MEMORY

What you work out in one session is gone by the next unless you remember it. A
memory is a key and a body, and the body is either prose or code — one slot, both
kinds, because in this language they are the same material.

- (memory/remember key body) store one. Prose in the body is something to know.
  A quoted form in the body is something to do, a recipe you run later. Quote it:
  the arguments are evaluated, so an unquoted form stores its result instead.
- (memory/recall query) retrieve by key or body text, strongest first. The query is
  a regular expression. What it finds is loaded into your context, so recalling IS
  reading and there is no echo step to write after it.
- (memories) every memory as key, score and trigger, strongest first.
- (memory/forget key) drop one for good.
- (memory/replay key) evaluate a memory whose body is a form, and return what it
  produced. A memory holding a defun installs that function when you replay it.
  This is how a remembered recipe becomes a running one.
- (memory/revise key body) correct a memory. See the window below.

The names are slashed because remember, recall and forget are ordinary English
verbs, and a bare one in an aside would be read as code.

FIRING BY ITSELF

With :on a memory watches for an event and loads itself when that event happens,
without you asking. The trigger is a quoted form of a kind and a pattern, and the
pattern is a regular expression. The seven kinds:

  call     a form about to be evaluated, matched on its head, at any depth —
           so it catches a call nested inside a setq or a let
  result   the printed value a top-level form returned
  error    an evaluation that raised, so a past mistake surfaces exactly as it
           is about to repeat
  step     every step
  prose    words in the prose around your forms
  user     words in what the user just said
  recall   another memory being retrieved

A memory set to fire on a call to the loader will surface what you know about a
server at the moment you load it. A memory set on an error will surface the fix
the moment the same error comes back.

A fired memory arrives as a line of REPL feedback, to you alone. It is not run and
the user does not see it. To run one, replay it. To show the user something, echo
it.

CHAINING

The last kind is the interesting one: retrieval is itself an event, so a memory can
hook another memory and the two arrive together. You can also say it outright with
:links, which takes a list of keys.

And it happens on its own: memories that fire in the same step get wired to each
other, so the things you keep using together start arriving together.

STRENGTH AND FORGETTING

Every memory has a score. Recalling one strengthens it. Leaving it alone lets it
fade — half its strength a week — and under a floor it is swept away. So what you
keep using stays and what you wrote once and never needed goes. Nothing has to be
tidied by hand, though memory/forget is there when something is simply wrong.

THE WINDOW

Retrieving a memory opens it: until the end of the step you can revise its body to
correct what it says. Outside that window revise is an error telling you to recall
it first. Reading and rewriting are one act on purpose — you fix a memory while you
are looking at it and can see that it was wrong.

GOTCHAS

- A memory body must be prose or a form that can be read back. A closure, a promise
  or a secret is a live value, prints in angle brackets, and remember rejects it.
  Store the quoted form that builds the thing, never the built thing itself.
- Quote a code body. Without the quote the form is evaluated and you store its
  result.
- memory/recall loads what it finds into your context. Do not follow it with an
  echo to see it properly; it is already in front of you.
- Firing does not run anything. A memory that fires on a call tells you what it
  knows; if it holds a recipe, replay it.
- Triggers watch the code you write into the REPL. A form evaluated inside a replay
  or inside a function body is not a step of its own and fires nothing.
- Memories outlive the interpreter. Definitions die on a reset and memories do not,
  so a recipe worth keeping belongs in a memory rather than in a defun you will
  lose.
- A memory with no :on never arrives by itself. It waits for a recall that names
  it, and if you cannot guess its key later it is as good as lost. Storing a
  correction without a trigger is the commonest way to write a memory that never
  helps you again.

RULES

- REMEMBER THE MOMENT A GUESS FAILS. A rejected call, a name that turned out wrong,
  an argument order you had backwards: that is the one thing worth keeping, and the
  moment you know it is the only moment you will ever have the correction in front
  of you. Write it before you move on, while the right answer is on screen.
- GIVE IT THE TRIGGER THAT WILL REPEAT, not a description of the topic. Ask what you
  will be doing when you next need this, and hook that. A tool you misnamed belongs
  on the call that loads its server, so it arrives before you can misname it again;
  a mistake the interpreter rejected belongs on that error. A memory you have to
  remember to look for is a memory you will not look for.
- Remember the correction, not the transcript. "browser_navigate, not navigate" is
  worth keeping; the whole tool listing is not, and the next listing will be
  different anyway.
- Do not remember what the REPL can tell you. Anything doc prints, anything a
  discovery call returns, anything you can recompute: look it up instead. Memory is
  for what you had to find out the hard way.
- Recall before you guess. When you are about to write a name you are not sure of
  and the session has not told you, that is the cheapest possible recall.
